Milestone Optimization for Brand Campaigns
1. Background
Meme'd is a creator marketplace. Creators (meme pages, clip accounts, hype pages) don't shoot original content; they pick a live brand campaign, cut clips/memes/edits from brand-supplied material, and post to Instagram or YouTube. Payment is not a flat rate. It's milestone-based: each campaign defines a ladder of view thresholds, and a creator's post unlocks a cash reward each time it crosses a threshold. Higher views unlock higher cumulative payouts.
Today, milestone ladders (e.g. "10K views → ₹500, 50K → ₹2,000, 100K → ₹5,000, 500K → ₹15,000") are set manually per campaign by ops/growth, largely on gut feel and whatever the brand's budget nominally allows. This creates real problems: some campaigns blow through budget because milestones were too generous relative to how far content actually reaches; others under-pay relative to what a similar campaign's content earned, causing creators to abandon a campaign mid-flight when the next milestone feels unreachable; ladders don't account for differences in content format (Reels vs. YouTube Shorts vs. long-form), creator audience size, or campaign category (gaming vs. FMCG vs. finance behave very differently); and there's no defensible answer to "why is milestone 3 set at 250K views and not 200K or 300K" when a brand asks.
2. The Problem
Design and implement a method that takes a new campaign's parameters (the parameters are for you to decide) and outputs a milestone ladder — a set of (view threshold, payout) pairs — that is "optimized." You get to define optimized, but your definition must be explicit, justified, and encoded in your method. At minimum, it should reason about the tradeoffs below; you don't need to solve all of them perfectly, but your write-up must show you've considered them and state what you chose to prioritize.
Budget adherence. Expected total payout across all participating creators, given realistic view distributions, should land within the brand's budget with a defined confidence level, not just in expectation.
Creator motivation and retention. Gaps between consecutive milestones shouldn't be so large that creators give up chasing the next one, especially early in a post's life, but also shouldn't be so small that the ladder is trivially climbed and cheap to grind for low-effort content.
Fairness across creator tiers. A campaign draws creators with wildly different audience sizes (a 5K-follower meme page vs. a 500K-follower one). A single ladder applied uniformly may be unreachable for small creators or trivial for large ones. Decide whether and how milestones should be tiered or normalized, and justify it.
Fraud/gaming resistance. View counts can be bought, boosted, or manipulated (bot views, engagement pods, paid promotion to hit a threshold). Your method should account for this risk somehow, either in how thresholds are set or in what data would be needed to detect it, even if full fraud detection is out of scope.
Marginal ROI to the brand. Payout per incremental view should make sense relative to what the brand would pay for that reach through other channels (e.g., paid media CPM). Milestones set without reference to this can end up paying creators far more, or far less, per view than the reach is worth.
3. Data
No Meme'd data, real or synthetic, is provided. You are responsible for generating your own synthetic dataset simulating campaign history, using the schema and relationships described below as a starting point (adjust as your method requires). The point of the exercise is the method and the reasoning behind it, not any particular dataset, so be explicit in your write-up about what distributional assumptions you baked into the data you generated, since that inevitably shapes your results.
campaigns: campaign_id, brand, category (gaming / FMCG / finance / D2C / entertainment), platform (instagram / youtube), total_budget, start_date, end_date, target_creator_tier (nano <10K / micro 10K-100K / mid 100K-1M / macro 1M+).
milestone_ladders (historical, as actually configured, the thing you're trying to improve on): campaign_id, milestone_rank, view_threshold, payout_amount.
creators: creator_id, platform, follower_count, tier, account_age_months, historical_avg_views_per_post, historical_completion_rate (share of past campaigns where they hit at least one milestone).
posts: post_id, campaign_id, creator_id, post_date, platform, format (reel / short / long_form / carousel), views_at_24h, views_at_7d, views_at_30d, views_final, total_payout_earned, flagged_suspicious (bool, a rough proxy for suspected view manipulation, already labeled for you).
Feel free to assume realistic distributions: views are heavy-tailed (a small share of posts drive most of the reach), follower count correlates with expected views but with high variance, and flagged_suspicious posts should show anomalous view-growth curves (e.g., a sudden spike inconsistent with the account's history).

4. What to Deliver
A. Methodology write-up. State your definition of "optimized" and how you weighted the tradeoffs. Describe your approach end to end, how you model expected view distribution for a new campaign given its parameters, how that translates into threshold placement, and how you set payout amounts at each threshold. Explain how the method adapts to a campaign that looks different from anything in the historical data (cold start). State the explicit assumptions and the limits of your approach, what would break it, what you'd want more data for.
B. Working code. Python/Typescript. Given a new campaign's parameters as input, your code should output a milestone ladder (view thresholds + payouts) as output. It should run on the synthetic data you generate or on a sample we provide, and it should be runnable by us, not just described in prose.
C. Backtest. Take 3-5 historical campaigns from your synthetic data. Run your method on their input parameters (pretending you don't know how they actually turned out) and compare the ladder your method proposes against the ladder that was actually used. Show, quantitatively, whether your method would have kept spending within budget, whether it would have paid out more or less than actual, and whether creator drop-off (proxied by completion rate) would plausibly have been better or worse. Include real numbers, not just a directional claim.
D. One page for a non-technical stakeholder. A brand manager or the founder needs to trust this without reading your code. Explain in plain language what the method does, why milestones set this way are better than the status quo, and where it might still get it wrong.
5. Evaluation Criteria
We're weighing these roughly evenly: whether the problem framing is right (did you correctly identify the tradeoffs and pick a defensible priority among them, rather than just building a curve-fit); statistical soundness (is the underlying model of view growth and distribution reasonable, and did you validate it rather than assert it); practical grounding (does the backtest actually show the method would have performed better, with real numbers); communication (can a non-technical stakeholder read and trust the system); and code quality (readable, runnable, not a notebook full of dead cells).
We are explicitly not grading on: use of any particular ML framework or algorithmic sophistication for its own sake, a perfectly polished UI, or handling every edge case. A well-reasoned method using a simple statistical model beats an over-engineered one you can't fully justify.
6. Constraints & Notes
The write-up and judgment need to be yours, and we'll ask you to walk through your reasoning live in the follow-up interview regardless. If any part of the brief is ambiguous, state the assumption you made rather than asking us to clarify (we want to see how you handle ambiguity).


